{%- comment -%}
  The size chart — a real table built from theme settings, not a pasted blob.

  Asked for 2026-09-13 with a screenshot of a competitor's size-guide panel:
  a table from XS to 3XL and a cm/inch switch. Two things about that screenshot
  were deliberately NOT copied, and both were agreed first:

  - **The COLUMNS are trousers, not the screenshot's.** The reference table was
    a top's — body length back, shoulder width seam to seam, body width, sleeve
    length centre back. Sixteen of the twenty products left on this store after
    the sold-out cull are trousers and culottes, and a shopper buying trousers
    measures a waist, not a shoulder. Shipping a top's chart on Yora Loose Pants
    would have been a wrong measurement stated confidently, which is the one
    class of content this repo refuses. The studio chose the format over the
    numbers; the numbers are theirs to send.

    **The top's numbers arrived on 2026-09-27**, and they did not fit the shape
    this paragraph assumed either. They came per GARMENT rather than per size —
    every top in the catalogue is one size, and none of the four carries a Size
    option in Shopify — so the tops chart's rows are pieces and its first column
    is headed Piece rather than Size. Nothing in the parsing below cares: the
    first cell is a row header whatever it names. See the Tops settings.
  - **It is inline, not a modal.** The same instruction that asked for this chart
    asked for the product page's four tabs to become one page. A dialog would
    have put a second thing to open back on a page that just had one taken off.

  WHERE THE DATA LIVES, and why it is a theme setting rather than a metafield.
  `custom.size_chart` still exists and still wins where a product has its own —
  see `product-detail.liquid`. What this renders is the SHARED chart, because
  Wear Label has one size system and eleven products cut to it: a metafield
  would mean the same table typed eleven times and eleven places to correct when
  one number is wrong. A theme setting is one field, in the theme editor, where
  every other piece of copy on this site already lives.

  IT TAKES A `chart` ARGUMENT NOW, added 2026-09-27 for the Size Guide page,
  which shows TWO charts on one page — trousers and tops. The product page's
  call is unchanged and still passes nothing, which still means trousers.

  IT NAMES A CHART RATHER THAN TAKING THE NUMBERS, and the first version did the
  opposite — it accepted `columns` and `rows` and fell back to the theme
  settings with `| default:`. THAT SHIPPED A WRONG TABLE and was caught on the
  rendered page: `default` replaces a BLANK value, not just a missing one, so
  the tops chart — whose rows are deliberately empty because the studio has
  never supplied top measurements — fell back to the TROUSER rows and rendered
  98 / 62-90 / 115 / 60 underneath the headings Length / Chest / Shoulder /
  Sleeve. A waist measurement presented as a chest, on a real product page, is
  exactly the class of content this repo refuses, and Liquid gave no hint: the
  page rendered, theme check passed, and the numbers looked plausible.

  Naming the chart removes the failure entirely rather than guarding against it.
  The snippet owns which settings it reads, so there is no argument that can be
  blank and no fallback to be wrong about.

  TWO CHARTS ON ONE PAGE NEEDED TWO FIXES THAT ARE EASY TO UNDO BY ACCIDENT:

  - **`uid`.** Radio buttons sharing a `name` outside a form are ONE group, so
    without this the tops chart's cm/inch switch would deselect the trouser
    chart's and the two would fight. Every caller on a page carrying more than
    one chart must pass a distinct `uid`.
  - **`data-size-guide` on the wrapper.** `initUnitSwitch` in assets/theme.js
    used to find its table with a document-wide `querySelector`, which returns
    the FIRST match on the page — so both switches would have driven the trouser
    table and the tops table would never have converted at all. The wrapper is
    the scope the script now searches inside. `data-unit-switch` deliberately
    stays on the fieldset rather than moving up to this wrapper, because
    theme-src/theme.css hides `[data-unit-switch]` under `scripting: none` and
    moving it would hide the whole chart from a reader with no script.

  THE SHAPE IS ONE TEXTAREA, ONE ROW PER LINE, PIPE SEPARATED:

      XS | 64 | 90 | 28 | 72 | 26
      S  | 68 | 94 | 28 | 72 | 27

  which is chosen so the studio can see the whole table at once and so that
  adding a size is a line, not a code change. It also keeps the size names out
  of this file — XS and 3XL are data, and a hardcoded user-visible string in a
  snippet is the rule this theme breaks least often.

  A ragged row is rendered ragged rather than padded or dropped. A missing cell
  is a number nobody has supplied, and silently filling it with an empty string
  the reader cannot tell from a measured zero is worse than a short row.

  CM TO INCHES IS COMPUTED, NOT TYPED. Every numeric cell carries `data-cm` and
  assets/theme.js rewrites the text when the switch moves; the studio types one
  set of numbers. Without script the switch is removed by the `scripting: none`
  block in theme-src/theme.css and the table stays in centimetres, which is the
  unit the studio measures in and the one the numbers are typed in.

  The wrapper is `.wl-table`, the same class the rich-text metafield route uses,
  so a chart from either source is styled once and scrolls sideways on a phone
  rather than forcing horizontal page scroll.

  Accepts:
    columns    - comma separated headings. Given WITH rows_array, this chart is
                 drawn instead of the store-wide one
    rows_array - an array of rows, each one pipe separated
    note       - the line under a passed-in chart
    uid        - unique per chart ON A PAGE CARRYING MORE THAN ONE (default 'main')

  With no columns it draws the store-wide trouser chart from theme settings.
  There is no longer a 'tops' variant of that: the four tops are one size each
  with their own figures, so they carry their own chart per product.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- comment -%}
  ROWS ARE SPLIT WITH `newline_to_br`, NOT ON A NEWLINE LITERAL, and that is not
  a style choice. A percent-brace liquid block is line-based: a quoted string
  carrying a real line break inside one ends the tag early, and theme check
  reports it as "expected end of input" pointing at the comment above rather
  than at the split. It cost a round trip here. `newline_to_br` turns the breaks
  into markup that can be split on a single line; both spellings are handled
  because the filter's output has been written each way.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  # NO `| default:` ON THE ROWS. See the note above: it treats blank and absent
  # as the same thing, and the tops rows are blank on purpose, so the fallback
  # silently painted trouser measurements under top headings. The branch below
  # reads one pair of settings or the other and never mixes them.
  # The note is per chart, not shared. That was the other way round for one
  # revision, on the reasoning that how a garment is measured is a studio
  # convention rather than a fact about trousers — true, and beside the point
  # once the tops figures arrived: what the tops chart needs to say is that its
  # pieces are all one size and that Nori fits to L, neither of which is true
  # of a trouser.
  # A CHART PASSED IN WINS OVER THE STORE-WIDE ONE, and that is what the tops
  # use now. Every top in this catalogue is one size with its own measurements,
  # so a shared table put four products' figures on one product's page. They
  # come from `custom.size_chart` per product instead; see product-detail.
  # `rows_array` is an array rather than a string because Liquid cannot rejoin
  # lines with a newline, and the caller has already had to split them to take
  # the heading row off the top.
  assign chart_uid = uid | default: 'main'
  assign has_rows = false

  if columns != blank and rows_array != blank
    assign columns_list = columns | split: ','
    assign rows_list = rows_array
    assign chart_note = note
    if rows_list.size > 0
      assign has_rows = true
    endif
  else
    assign chart_columns = settings.size_guide_columns
    assign chart_rows = settings.size_guide_rows
    assign chart_note = settings.size_guide_note

    assign columns_list = chart_columns | split: ','

    assign row_source = chart_rows | strip | newline_to_br
    assign row_source = row_source | replace: '<br />', '|~|' | replace: '<br>', '|~|'
    assign rows_list = row_source | split: '|~|'

    if chart_rows != blank and rows_list.size > 0
      assign has_rows = true
    endif
  endif

  assign unit_name = 'size-unit-' | append: chart_uid
-%}

{%- if has_rows -%}
  <div data-size-guide class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    {%- comment -%}
      Radios rather than two buttons: the unit is one choice out of two, which is
      what a radio group is, and it arrives keyboard-operable with arrow keys for
      free. `data-unit-switch` is the hook; the fieldset is what `scripting: none`
      removes. `data-unit-option` is what the script matches on, rather than the
      control's name, because the name now varies from chart to chart.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <fieldset data-unit-switch class="flex items-center gap-5">
      <legend class="sr-only">{{ 'product.units' | t }}</legend>

      <label class="inline-flex min-h-11 cursor-pointer items-center gap-2 text-caption uppercase tracking-nav text-ink-muted has-checked:text-ink">
        <input type="radio" data-unit-option name="{{ unit_name }}" value="cm" checked class="size-4 accent-brand">
        {{ 'product.cm' | t }}
      </label>

      <label class="inline-flex min-h-11 cursor-pointer items-center gap-2 text-caption uppercase tracking-nav text-ink-muted has-checked:text-ink">
        <input type="radio" data-unit-option name="{{ unit_name }}" value="in" class="size-4 accent-brand">
        {{ 'product.inch' | t }}
      </label>
    </fieldset>

    <div class="wl-table">
      <table data-size-table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            {%- for column in columns_list -%}
              <th scope="col">{{ column | strip }}</th>
            {%- endfor -%}
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {%- for row in rows_list -%}
            {%- assign cells = row | split: '|' -%}
            <tr>
              {%- for cell in cells -%}
                {%- assign value = cell | strip -%}
                {%- if forloop.first -%}
                  {%- comment -%}
                    The first cell is the size name, so it is a row header and it
                    is never converted — "XS" is not a measurement.
                  {%- endcomment -%}
                  <th scope="row">{{ value }}</th>
                {%- else -%}
                  {%- comment -%}
                    `data-cm` is what makes the cell convertible. A cell that is
                    not a number keeps none, so script leaves it alone — a dash or
                    a note in the middle of a table survives the switch.
                  {%- endcomment -%}
                  {%- comment -%}
                    EVERY measurement cell carries `data-cm`, and the decision
                    about whether it converts is left to script. The first pass
                    gated this on `value | times: 1 > 0`, which looks right and
                    silently breaks a RANGE: "62-90" casts to 62, so the cell
                    would have been relabelled 24.4 in inches and the range would
                    have been thrown away. theme.js rewrites each number it finds
                    inside the string instead, so "62-90" becomes "24.4-35.4" and
                    a cell holding a dash or a note is left alone because it
                    holds no digits at all.
                  {%- endcomment -%}
                  <td data-cm="{{ value }}">{{ value }}</td>
                {%- endif -%}
              {%- endfor -%}
            </tr>
          {%- endfor -%}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    {%- if chart_note != blank -%}
      <p class="text-caption leading-snug text-ink-muted">{{ chart_note }}</p>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
{%- else -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    No rows typed yet. The labelled placeholder at final size, like every other
    empty slot on this site — the layout is finished before the numbers arrive.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="wl-table">
    {% render 'copy', value: blank, label: 'size chart', lines: 4 %}
  </div>
{%- endif -%}
